1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,634
Level 6 priced one decision: a point outside three

2
00:00:04,734 --> 00:00:08,490
sigma. Nought point two seven percent of the time

3
00:00:08,590 --> 00:00:12,268
the process is fine and the chart shouts anyway.

4
00:00:13,466 --> 00:00:17,945
Now move the process. A real shift of one sigma — and watch

5
00:00:18,045 --> 00:00:22,990
where it goes. Almost the whole curve is still inside the limits.

6
00:00:23,083 --> 00:00:27,010
Which means the chance of catching it on the next point

7
00:00:27,110 --> 00:00:31,184
is about two percent. Beta — the chance of staying silent

8
00:00:31,284 --> 00:00:35,065
— is nought point nine seven seven. That is the error

9
00:00:35,165 --> 00:00:38,727
Level 6 never mentioned, and it is the bigger one.

10
00:00:41,116 --> 00:00:44,613
Put that on an axis. How big does a shift have

11
00:00:44,713 --> 00:00:47,976
to be before one point is likely to see it?

12
00:00:48,566 --> 00:00:53,259
At one sigma, two percent. At two sigma, sixteen. The chart

13
00:00:53,359 --> 00:00:57,809
is not broken — a small shift simply looks like ordinary

14
00:00:57,909 --> 00:01:01,546
noise to a test that sees one point at a time.

15
00:01:01,633 --> 00:01:06,359
It takes a shift of three full sigma — the mean landing exactly

16
00:01:06,459 --> 00:01:10,725
on the limit — before the next point is even a coin toss.

17
00:01:10,816 --> 00:01:14,449
And that is not a coincidence: when the mean sits on

18
00:01:14,549 --> 00:01:18,396
the limit, half the distribution is on each side of it.

19
00:01:18,483 --> 00:01:22,115
One point, one chance. Everything the rules do

20
00:01:22,215 --> 00:01:25,199
next is an attempt to get around that.

21
00:01:27,583 --> 00:01:30,882
Here is a chart doing its job. Every point

22
00:01:30,982 --> 00:01:34,443
inside the limits, so every point is a pass.

23
00:01:36,133 --> 00:01:39,794
Except this one. Two point five sigma — inside

24
00:01:39,894 --> 00:01:43,473
the limits, so the chart calls it in control.

25
00:01:43,566 --> 00:01:47,047
But ask how surprising it is and the answer is

26
00:01:47,147 --> 00:01:51,094
one in eighty. Anywhere else in statistics that is a

27
00:01:51,194 --> 00:01:54,986
finding. Here it is filed as a pass and forgotten.

28
00:01:55,083 --> 00:01:59,072
A verdict is not the same as the evidence. The extra

29
00:01:59,172 --> 00:02:03,239
rules exist to spend what a single point cannot hold.

30
00:02:05,633 --> 00:02:10,328
So here are the four Western Electric rules. Usually taught as

31
00:02:10,428 --> 00:02:13,885
a list to memorise. They are not a list — they

32
00:02:13,985 --> 00:02:17,365
are four purchases, and each one has a price.

33
00:02:17,450 --> 00:02:22,940
Rule one alone: a false alarm every 377 subgroups,

34
00:02:23,040 --> 00:02:26,854
and a one sigma shift caught in 43.

35
00:02:26,950 --> 00:02:30,186
Add rule two — two of three points beyond

36
00:02:30,286 --> 00:02:33,930
two sigma on the same side. Both numbers fall.

37
00:02:34,016 --> 00:02:39,172
Rule three, four of five beyond one sigma. They fall again.

38
00:02:39,266 --> 00:02:42,027
And rule four: eight in a row on one

39
00:02:42,127 --> 00:02:45,046
side of the line, no matter how close.

40
00:02:45,133 --> 00:02:50,212
All four together: a false alarm every 91 subgroups. That

41
00:02:50,312 --> 00:02:55,664
number was published in 1987 as ninety-one point seven five,

42
00:02:55,764 --> 00:02:59,481
and this simulation was not told about it.

43
00:03:01,966 --> 00:03:06,277
Line up what it cost against what it bought. The cost is

44
00:03:06,377 --> 00:03:11,082
one number and it never changes: four times the false alarms.

45
00:03:11,666 --> 00:03:16,381
Against a one sigma drift the rules catch it four and a half times

46
00:03:16,481 --> 00:03:20,758
sooner. That is above the cost line, and it is a good trade.

47
00:03:20,850 --> 00:03:24,799
Against a three sigma jump, they catch it barely sooner

48
00:03:24,899 --> 00:03:28,038
at all — because rule one already sees it on

49
00:03:28,138 --> 00:03:31,646
the next point. Same cost, almost nothing bought.

50
00:03:31,733 --> 00:03:35,908
So the rules are not good or bad. The cost is fixed and the

51
00:03:36,008 --> 00:03:41,271
benefit is whatever shift you are actually afraid of — which is a question

52
00:03:41,371 --> 00:03:46,633
about your process, and the arithmetic is only here to make it answerable.

